		<profileDesc>
			<abstract>
				<p>We present a comprehensive study on meaningfully evaluating sign language utterances in the form of human skeletal poses. The study covers keypoint distance-based, embeddingbased, and back-translation-based metrics. We show tradeoffs between different metrics in different scenarios through (1) automatic meta-evaluation of sign-level retrieval, and (2) a human correlation study of textto-pose translation across different sign languages. Our findings, along with the opensource pose-evaluation toolkit, provide a practical and reproducible approach for developing and evaluating sign language translation or generation systems.</p>
			</abstract>
